# The only contents the contamination gate will skip.
#
# Four files exist to contain the thing the gate looks for: two synthetic
# fixtures, the hashed denylist and the shape rules. The pass two gate
# exempted them **by path**, which exempted whatever anyone later put in
# them. Round four demonstrated it: append a denied term to the canary and
# the gate reported clean.
#
# So the exemption is of the approved bytes, not of the name. Each line is
# the sha256 of the approved contents. If a file's bytes differ from its
# digest here, the gate scans it like any other file, and the denied terms it
# legitimately contains will fail the build until somebody updates this line
# on purpose.
#
# Changing a fixture is therefore two deliberate edits, not one:
#
#   shasum -a 256 tests/gate/canary.txt
#
# and paste the result here. `pnpm gate:selftest` fails if any line is stale,
# so this cannot drift quietly.
#
# Format: <sha256>  <repository-relative path>

# Every approved version, not only the current one. These files legitimately
# change: a canary gains a phrase, the denylist gains a term. Each edit is an
# approval, and the old bytes stay approved because they are still in the
# history the pre-push scan reads. Approving only the current version would
# make every past fixture a permanent violation.
#
# tests/gate/canary.txt
37134e7ca4353856779420a5b1ea2fb1dccddbaf314bb8d8b5812e54cd1f86f7  tests/gate/canary.txt
7a5b9489626c5dc8d7f7bae636ea41c87352fe4cf43f1e2a3bbee8cb7c11d829  tests/gate/canary.txt

